1
00:00:15,174 --> 00:00:19,877
[SPEAKER_02]: You also read many times that citta nimitta is important.

2
00:00:20,517 --> 00:00:22,878
[SPEAKER_02]: It's important to see your mind, to understand your mind.

3
00:00:23,559 --> 00:00:29,062
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how, what is the best environment for that?

4
00:00:29,942 --> 00:00:31,583
[SPEAKER_02]: Being able to see your mind.

5
00:00:33,424 --> 00:00:36,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-sensuality, non-distraction, non-company.

6
00:00:38,908 --> 00:00:44,731
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't see your mind when you're just constantly in the company, when most of your day is spent in the company of others.

7
00:00:45,272 --> 00:00:51,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if they're all perfectly silent, perfectly mindful on the retreat, still, it's others.

8
00:00:52,317 --> 00:00:58,842
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if one wants to see the sign of the mind, one needs to develop perceptions of solitude.

9
00:00:58,982 --> 00:01:08,148
[SPEAKER_01]: One needs to be secluded from sensuality, in terms of his... not just his senses, but his mind as well.

10
00:01:08,168 --> 00:01:11,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's another thing, like...

11
00:01:11,350 --> 00:01:13,892
[SPEAKER_01]: It's an exercise somebody can do, if they're not sure what it's meant by this.

12
00:01:13,952 --> 00:01:14,913
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, imagine

13
00:01:15,630 --> 00:01:25,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Imagine you, you sort of, eh... you go to the unknown area, some forest, some wild jungle, and you go there with ten people, as a group.

14
00:01:26,173 --> 00:01:31,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, imagine, like, what, what is, like, the basic, the basis mood, basis of your mood, how you're feeling at the time.

15
00:01:33,455 --> 00:01:33,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's fine.

16
00:01:34,495 --> 00:01:43,818
[SPEAKER_01]: Not necessarily how I'm feeling pleasant, unpleasant, just, just overall, what is the sense, what is the overall perception, as well, of this group of us being in, in this environment.

17
00:01:45,053 --> 00:02:08,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, for the comparison's sake, you go to the equally remote, unknown environment, all alone, and then compare the difference in the basis of that perception, of that experience as a whole, and you'll see that there is something fundamentally different when you perceive yourself alone, and when you perceive yourself with at least one more person, or more and more.

18
00:02:09,857 --> 00:02:13,900
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's that difference, that's the basis for the right practice, that solitude.

19
00:02:14,461 --> 00:02:20,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, once you discern that correctly, then, sure, even when you are with others, you would be alone.

20
00:02:20,826 --> 00:02:26,331
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't discern that, means even when you are alone, you are actually with others.

21
00:02:28,813 --> 00:02:36,379
[SPEAKER_01]: So, on the basis of that recognition of that existential solitude, obviously, sense restraint and virtue developed.

22
00:02:37,113 --> 00:02:45,596
[SPEAKER_01]: You start developing, you start discerning that sense of the general, fundamentally enduring mood you're in.

23
00:02:46,377 --> 00:02:47,797
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what mind is, basically.

24
00:02:48,178 --> 00:02:55,401
[SPEAKER_01]: Practically speaking, closest to what citta is, would be the most general mood of your currently enduring situation.

25
00:02:55,461 --> 00:02:59,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, oh, I feel something in my back, or my knee hurts.

26
00:02:59,182 --> 00:02:59,322
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

27
00:02:59,502 --> 00:03:00,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Overall mood.

28
00:03:01,123 --> 00:03:01,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it agreeable?

29
00:03:01,923 --> 00:03:02,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it disagreeable?

30
00:03:02,904 --> 00:03:03,624
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it elated?

31
00:03:03,664 --> 00:03:04,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it depressed?

32
00:03:04,524 --> 00:03:05,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it sort of coming?

33
00:03:05,425 --> 00:03:06,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it sort of going?

34
00:03:06,765 --> 00:03:07,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it increasing?

35
00:03:07,606 --> 00:03:08,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it fading?

36
00:03:08,827 --> 00:03:09,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it lust?

37
00:03:09,648 --> 00:03:10,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it anger?

38
00:03:10,549 --> 00:03:11,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it neither?

39
00:03:11,409 --> 00:03:12,330
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it equanimity?

40
00:03:13,512 --> 00:03:15,473
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how the Buddha described the mind as well.

41
00:03:16,374 --> 00:03:18,356
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's, it's, it's...

42
00:03:18,396 --> 00:03:26,284
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot be described more accurately than in those general terms, which for some people might be a bit ambiguous, because they're not used to discerning it like that.

43
00:03:26,684 --> 00:03:29,547
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, they're not used to discerning what their mind is.

44
00:03:33,486 --> 00:03:33,986
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

45
00:03:34,686 --> 00:03:46,531
[SPEAKER_01]: You basically, you need a certain basis of virtue, solitude, and then that the signs of this enduring mood, regardless of what's particularly happening, are becoming more and more visible.

46
00:03:46,951 --> 00:03:52,613
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the signs of the mood, the signs of the mind, not the mind.

47
00:03:52,953 --> 00:03:53,813
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, exactly.

48
00:03:54,034 --> 00:03:55,374
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not, oh, that's my mind.

49
00:03:55,794 --> 00:04:00,516
[SPEAKER_01]: You see the signs and tokens of the mind, on account of which you get to discern the mind.

50
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:13,863
[SPEAKER_02]: Good one is, I'm having lustful thoughts.

51
00:04:14,043 --> 00:04:16,265
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm having angry, complaining thoughts.

52
00:04:16,285 --> 00:04:17,186
[SPEAKER_02]: Thoughts of complaining.

53
00:04:19,487 --> 00:04:30,175
[SPEAKER_02]: And from that, knowing that I'm having... knowing that you are actually thinking thoughts of complaining, you know... Yeah, so that's like the first step.

54
00:04:30,195 --> 00:04:32,577
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize, okay, my mind is affected with complaining.

55
00:04:33,077 --> 00:04:35,119
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't try to stop that or get rid of it.

56
00:04:36,138 --> 00:04:37,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Nor indulge it, for that matter.

57
00:04:37,459 --> 00:04:38,299
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not feeling it either.

58
00:04:38,699 --> 00:04:40,099
[SPEAKER_01]: You look what's the basis of it.

59
00:04:40,220 --> 00:04:42,140
[SPEAKER_01]: So, my mind is here, it is complaining.

60
00:04:42,420 --> 00:04:48,202
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what's... what underlines, simultaneously, this experience of complaining mind?

61
00:04:49,523 --> 00:04:50,663
[SPEAKER_01]: What underlines it?

62
00:04:50,843 --> 00:04:51,564
[SPEAKER_01]: It's discomfort.

63
00:04:52,644 --> 00:04:53,624
[SPEAKER_01]: It's unpleasant.

64
00:04:53,644 --> 00:04:54,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Something is unpleasant.

65
00:04:55,745 --> 00:05:00,527
[SPEAKER_01]: You feel displeasure in regard to something, hence the symptom of complaining.

66
00:05:01,478 --> 00:05:08,404
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, you're not concerned with complaining as an issue, you start discerning it as a sign and token, i.e.

67
00:05:08,624 --> 00:05:14,969
[SPEAKER_01]: as a symptom of the underlying issue of the mind trying to avoid this pleasure.

68
00:05:16,770 --> 00:05:19,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Same with anger, same with anything, basically.

69
00:05:19,633 --> 00:05:21,274
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether it's sensuality, again,

70
00:05:22,022 --> 00:05:22,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, mind wants this.

71
00:05:23,663 --> 00:05:30,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so those are... You can take that as an issue in itself, as an object in itself, and then try to get rid of it, try to think the opposite of this.

72
00:05:31,186 --> 00:05:31,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Good luck with that.

73
00:05:32,666 --> 00:05:46,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you could start... What if I take this as an indication of an underlying problem, which is the state of mind that I'm not aware of, and thus reacting to it, giving in to the pressure of that mind, of that mood.

74
00:05:47,358 --> 00:05:55,380
[SPEAKER_02]: But what you were saying now, it's also sort of indicating that the mind is not a pure, good thing.

75
00:05:57,180 --> 00:05:58,440
[SPEAKER_01]: It isn't, unless you develop it.

76
00:06:00,821 --> 00:06:05,882
[SPEAKER_01]: And certainly for somebody, for Putrajana, the mind is always not pure, because it's always affected with wrong view.

77
00:06:06,922 --> 00:06:11,563
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why within that wrongness, Putrajana needs to start discerning what is less wrong.

78
00:06:13,703 --> 00:06:17,904
[SPEAKER_01]: And what... what is less wrong than greed, aversion, delusion, is non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion.

79
00:06:17,924 --> 00:06:19,804
[SPEAKER_01]: But yes, he's still affected with the wrong view.

80
00:06:20,424 --> 00:06:25,445
[SPEAKER_01]: But because he found something less wrong, within that, he can go even further and make that less wrong.

81
00:06:26,085 --> 00:06:34,147
[SPEAKER_01]: When eventually, you abandon all the wrong, crudely wrong, or things that are less wrong, that's how you develop the right view.

82
00:06:35,647 --> 00:06:38,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Through understanding the... the fundamental nature of the wrong view.

83
00:06:39,328 --> 00:06:40,148
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your right view.

84
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:46,241
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you would discern... you would discern the starting point is... is wrong.

85
00:06:47,321 --> 00:06:54,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you took instruction on the degree of faith and so on, and you start practicing reflection, and then you start discerning things that are less wrong.

86
00:06:54,483 --> 00:06:55,863
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's all about undoing.

87
00:06:57,043 --> 00:07:00,484
[SPEAKER_01]: And in order to keep undoing, you need to keep seeing further and further.

88
00:07:00,524 --> 00:07:02,384
[SPEAKER_01]: What... what's... what's this underlined by?

89
00:07:02,824 --> 00:07:04,005
[SPEAKER_01]: What's this underlined by?

90
00:07:04,685 --> 00:07:10,606
[SPEAKER_01]: But the problem with Puthujjana's mind in general is, he takes for granted that what he thinks it's right, it's right.

91
00:07:11,520 --> 00:07:13,982
[SPEAKER_01]: While he at the same time knows he's not a sotāpanna.

92
00:07:15,964 --> 00:07:21,209
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have no such criteria, which means, where you think it's right, you should not regard as right necessarily.

93
00:07:21,469 --> 00:07:22,990
[SPEAKER_01]: You should look what's underneath it.

94
00:07:23,931 --> 00:07:25,433
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha would say, delve with a knife.

95
00:07:25,573 --> 00:07:27,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Keep cutting in and cutting in and cutting in.

96
00:07:27,595 --> 00:07:32,059
[SPEAKER_01]: Not keep building more and more and more until you get a perfect system that you built in your mind.

97
00:07:34,781 --> 00:07:36,323
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you cannot start rightly,

98
00:07:37,347 --> 00:07:41,388
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't mean you have to stay with that wrongness, so to speak.

99
00:07:42,308 --> 00:07:47,749
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't start rightly, but you can start less wrongly, and less wrongly, and less wrongly.

100
00:07:49,169 --> 00:07:50,769
[SPEAKER_01]: And just, back to...

101
00:07:51,189 --> 00:07:51,789
[SPEAKER_01]: The sign of the mind.

102
00:07:51,809 --> 00:07:52,530
[SPEAKER_01]: The sign of the mind.

103
00:07:53,150 --> 00:07:59,651
[SPEAKER_02]: You're saying that that mind is interested... Well, that will become apparent.

104
00:07:59,851 --> 00:08:00,051
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

105
00:08:00,211 --> 00:08:00,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

106
00:08:00,671 --> 00:08:03,071
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start discerning... That's the problem.

107
00:08:03,091 --> 00:08:06,212
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever's said for Puthujjana, he jumps at it.

108
00:08:07,603 --> 00:08:10,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever he perceives through senses, that's where his attention goes.

109
00:08:11,844 --> 00:08:14,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever mood arises, that's where his attention is.

110
00:08:15,065 --> 00:08:19,546
[SPEAKER_01]: What I'm saying is, you want to stop being concerned with particular problems.

111
00:08:20,166 --> 00:08:29,088
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to start regarding them as indications of the much broader, much more fundamental liability to the problem in the first place.

112
00:08:30,309 --> 00:08:35,030
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, as I said, in the example of the complaining mind,

113
00:08:35,420 --> 00:08:36,741
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you might stop complaining.

114
00:08:36,761 --> 00:08:38,122
[SPEAKER_01]: I might watch my breath, watch my eyes.

115
00:08:38,742 --> 00:08:39,402
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you calm down.

116
00:08:39,502 --> 00:08:39,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

117
00:08:40,363 --> 00:08:41,404
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's just management.

118
00:08:42,504 --> 00:08:54,131
[SPEAKER_01]: But instead of that, when complaining is present, while complaining is present, through not engaging with complaining, but not trying to get rid of it either, you start to see, what is this a symptom of?

119
00:08:55,592 --> 00:08:58,033
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you stop regarding symptoms as your illness.

120
00:08:58,313 --> 00:08:59,294
[SPEAKER_01]: You start seeing them as

121
00:09:00,060 --> 00:09:04,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Indication of a much broader, less visible illness.

122
00:09:04,925 --> 00:09:10,790
[SPEAKER_01]: And that illness is mind affected with greed, mind affected with aversion, mind affected with delusion.

123
00:09:11,371 --> 00:09:20,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you start seeing it on that level, like indirectly, so to speak, to begin with, you recognize, oh, it's because the mind has arisen with this lust.

124
00:09:21,220 --> 00:09:23,202
[SPEAKER_01]: These are the thoughts that are harassing me.

125
00:09:24,030 --> 00:09:26,891
[SPEAKER_01]: But not because I want the thoughts, I must get rid of the thoughts.

126
00:09:27,652 --> 00:09:29,753
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you stop being affected by either.

127
00:09:30,513 --> 00:09:32,434
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize... and that's satipaṭṭhāna sutta.

128
00:09:32,514 --> 00:09:36,476
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows elated mind as elated mind, depressed mind as depressed mind.

129
00:09:37,377 --> 00:09:38,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the mind is depressed now.

130
00:09:38,698 --> 00:09:39,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Not my problem.

131
00:09:39,898 --> 00:09:42,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Depressed mind has a corresponding symptom of these type of thoughts.

132
00:09:43,260 --> 00:09:44,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Not my problem either.

133
00:09:45,561 --> 00:09:47,422
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you overcome your thinking.

134
00:09:47,522 --> 00:09:53,165
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by trying to get rid of it or controlling it, but by seeing what the basis of it is and abandoning it.

135
00:09:58,709 --> 00:10:07,171
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't delight in what mind offers, you don't try to get rid of it, you start seeing it as an indication, as symptoms of the state of the mind that's currently present.

136
00:10:07,191 --> 00:10:18,015
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you would not... it's unthinkable, it's impossible that the mind, say, is in a nonsensical mood,

137
00:10:18,920 --> 00:10:21,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, your perceptions are perceptions of sensuality.

138
00:10:21,241 --> 00:10:22,441
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

139
00:10:22,461 --> 00:10:26,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Or the mind is free from aversion and yet you have thoughts of ill-will.

140
00:10:26,343 --> 00:10:27,103
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

141
00:10:27,803 --> 00:10:30,064
[SPEAKER_01]: So, thoughts of ill-will means mind of aversion.

142
00:10:30,724 --> 00:10:35,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Thoughts of sensuality means... thoughts of sensual pleasures mean mind of sensuality.

143
00:10:36,360 --> 00:10:41,983
[SPEAKER_01]: Thoughts of distraction, carelessness, whatever means, mind of basically indolence and delusion.

144
00:10:43,044 --> 00:10:44,945
[SPEAKER_02]: So, being aware of your thoughts.

145
00:10:45,665 --> 00:10:46,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Rightly.

146
00:10:46,225 --> 00:10:47,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Having right attitude.

147
00:10:47,506 --> 00:10:49,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just, I'm aware of what I'm thinking.

148
00:10:49,287 --> 00:10:53,629
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to start learning how to regard them as an indication of the state of mind.

149
00:10:53,709 --> 00:11:00,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just, oh, this is sensuality, this is lust, this is lust, it's all impermanent, it's all impermanent, I must look at the ugly, look at the ugly.

150
00:11:00,633 --> 00:11:00,773
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

151
00:11:00,813 --> 00:11:05,796
[SPEAKER_01]: You must see the basis of your sensual thoughts, which is the mind affected by sensuality.

152
00:11:06,376 --> 00:11:07,836
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you approve the sensual thoughts.

153
00:11:14,178 --> 00:11:16,458
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why the signs and tokens of the mind.

154
00:11:17,539 --> 00:11:19,839
[SPEAKER_01]: And for that, as we said, virtue is necessary.

155
00:11:19,859 --> 00:11:25,421
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to stop engaging at face value with the prospect of what your senses offer you.

156
00:11:25,481 --> 00:11:28,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Prospect of pleasure, prospect of rejection, prospect of denial.

157
00:11:29,502 --> 00:11:32,282
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop engaging with that, then those thoughts become more apparent.

158
00:11:32,882 --> 00:11:34,683
[SPEAKER_01]: Thoughts of, I want to do this, I want to say this.

159
00:11:35,523 --> 00:11:43,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, if you stay with that and don't act, don't try to get rid of it, then you start seeing, ah, all of this is because the mind is enduring in such and such state.

160
00:11:44,450 --> 00:11:47,472
[SPEAKER_01]: State of sensuality, state of aversion, state of delusion.

161
00:11:47,532 --> 00:11:48,353
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't want it.

162
00:11:48,853 --> 00:11:49,433
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want.

163
00:11:49,654 --> 00:11:56,559
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want it, but you realize if you say yes or no to the symptoms, you lose the sight of the illness, which means you're affected by it.

164
00:11:58,140 --> 00:12:04,585
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to truly uproot it is to develop this passion towards that mind that you discern through these indications of it.

165
00:12:05,938 --> 00:12:07,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Not through management and control of your thinking.

166
00:12:07,979 --> 00:12:08,719
[SPEAKER_01]: It's almost a relief.

167
00:12:08,859 --> 00:12:10,520
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's a huge relief.

168
00:12:10,580 --> 00:12:11,520
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a huge relief.

169
00:12:11,540 --> 00:12:13,821
[SPEAKER_01]: You go beyond, basically.

170
00:12:13,861 --> 00:12:15,722
[SPEAKER_01]: You fully cannot be reached completely.

171
00:12:16,202 --> 00:12:19,603
[SPEAKER_01]: You go beyond the domain of complaining thoughts or sensual thoughts.

172
00:12:20,764 --> 00:12:26,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Because those are all indications of what the mind... That's why developing your mind results in first jhāna.

173
00:12:26,186 --> 00:12:30,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Developing your mind results in development of samādhi.

174
00:12:30,407 --> 00:12:31,608
[SPEAKER_01]: Results in imperturbable.

175
00:12:37,820 --> 00:12:43,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if... if you have treated the illness, can any symptom apply to you ever again of that illness?

176
00:12:44,104 --> 00:12:44,264
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

177
00:12:44,744 --> 00:12:45,585
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable.

178
00:12:48,166 --> 00:12:50,348
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's seclusion, cittaviveka?

179
00:12:50,828 --> 00:12:54,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Cittaviveka would be the kind of the culmination.

180
00:12:54,990 --> 00:12:55,911
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, that's what I mean.

181
00:12:56,191 --> 00:13:02,355
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to be sense-restrained and virtuous, then you actually need to be physically secluded.

182
00:13:03,797 --> 00:13:13,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Most of the time, to start discerning, like seclusion, seclusion, seclusion of my senses from its object, then seclusion of my mind from my thoughts.

183
00:13:14,889 --> 00:13:21,116
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you are not even secluded from your sense objects of a perception of others, yeah, good luck secluding your mind from your thoughts.

